// What a matrix's cells have to be — and the one thing about them that is this program's choice
// rather than the library's.
//
// Linear algebra needs a *field*: a type that adds, subtracts, multiplies, divides, negates, has an
// additive and a multiplicative identity, and can be compared. Nine of those ten arrive as core
// traits, and `Zero` and `One` arrive because a generic body cannot spell an identity any other way —
// `0.0` is a `real` and nothing else, so a body shared by two element types has to *ask*.
//
// The tenth is the pivot. Gaussian elimination chooses the largest remaining cell in a column, so it
// needs a **magnitude**: an ordering on size that is not an ordering on values. The complexes have no
// `<` at all — the library refuses `Ord` for them on purpose, because there is no order on the plane
// that respects arithmetic — and yet `|z|` is exactly what pivoting wants.
//
// **That tenth requirement is `sysl.math.Magnitude`, and it used to be declared here.** The reason it
// could not be the library's was that a magnitude has to say what it answers *with*, and a library
// trait would have had to fix that for everybody — `real`, which is a floating-point commitment made
// on behalf of element types with no floating point in them. An associated type is what removed the
// obstacle: `Magnitude` declares `type Size: Ord` and each implementation fills it, so a `Complex[F]`
// measures in `F` and an integer measures in itself. This program asks for the trait and implements
// none of it; the three memberships it uses are the library's own.
//
// **What is left here is one member, and it is a choice rather than a gap.** A magnitude is compared
// against other magnitudes, which is all `gauss.sysl` does with one. Everything *else* this program
// measures — the length of a vector, how close two results are, the tolerance a check is written
// against — is reported as a `real`, because a length is a real number and a literal tolerance has to
// be written at some width. `norm` is that view, and each element type says how to take it.

import sysl.math.Float
import sysl.math.Magnitude
import sysl.math.complex.Complex

// The element type of a vector or a matrix. Every requirement is used: `gauss.sysl` divides, negates,
// compares against a zero and multiplies pivots into a determinant, `Magnitude` is what it pivots on,
// and `text` renders.
//
// `Display` is not algebra and is here anyway, because a numerical type that cannot be printed is a
// numerical type nobody can debug — and a bound is where a generic body says what it needs, whatever
// the need is for.
trait Scalar: Add + Sub + Mul + Div + Neg + Zero + One + Eq + Display + Magnitude

    // The size of this value as a `real`: the same quantity `magnitude` gives, in the one width this
    // program prints and writes its tolerances at.
    norm(self) -> real

impl Scalar for real
    norm(self) -> real = self.magnitude()

// The same size at the narrower width, widened at the end rather than at the start: `f32.magnitude()`
// is an `f32`, and widening a size that has already been taken loses nothing. Note which comparisons
// this does *not* reach — `gauss.sysl` pivots on the `f32` sizes themselves.
impl Scalar for f32
    norm(self) -> real = real(self.magnitude())

// The integers, and the reason this program has a second elimination.
//
// **Every requirement above is met outright**, including `Div` — so `Matrix[int]` compiles, `solve`
// runs on it, and the answers are wrong. `Zero` and `One` are the library's, `magnitude` reaches the
// whole integer family through one blanket block, and the size of an integer is an integer, so the
// only member left to supply is the view of that size as a `real`.
//
// **That the bound is satisfied and the algorithm is still wrong is this program's sharpest finding.**
// A bound is a list of operations, not a list of laws: nothing in `Scalar` can say that `/` returns
// what it was asked for. See `bareiss.sysl` for the elimination that needs no such promise.
impl Scalar for int
    norm(self) -> real = real(self.magnitude())

// The case the whole exercise is for. `Complex[F]` measures in `F` — the modulus is a *real* number
// however the parts are stored — so this is one conversion and no arithmetic, at every float width
// the library has.
impl[F: Float + Display] Scalar for Complex[F]
    norm(self) -> real = real(self.magnitude())
